@page "/Error"
@using System.Diagnostics

<PageTitle>Something went wrong — DMARC Monitor</PageTitle>

@*
    What UseExceptionHandler serves for anything unhandled. It was the
    scaffolded Blazor template: styled with Bootstrap classes this app does
    not define, and telling the reader to switch the application into
    Development mode - which DEPLOYING.md, and the template's own next
    sentence, say not to do on a deployed app. Advice to turn off a safety
    measure is the last thing to put in front of somebody whose instance has
    just fallen over.

    Nothing about the fault itself is shown: an exception message can carry a
    connection string or a customer's address. The request id is the handle
    for finding it in the log, which is where the detail belongs.
*@

<header class="page-head">
    <div>
        <h1>Something went wrong</h1>
        <p class="sub">This page could not be built. Nothing was changed, and no customer's DNS
           was touched — writes only happen on a button that says so.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="empty">
    <h2>What to do</h2>
    <p>Go back and try again. If it happens again, whoever runs this instance can find what
       happened in the log:</p>
    <p><code>sudo journalctl -u dmarc-web -n 200 --no-pager</code></p>

    @if (ShowRequestId)
    {
        <p>
            Quote this, which marks the exact entry:<br />
            <code>@RequestId</code>
        </p>
    }

    <p class="sub">The detail is deliberately not shown here. An error message can contain a
       database path, a token or a customer's address, and this page is reachable by anyone
       who can reach the application.</p>
</div>

@code{
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private string? RequestId { get; set; }
    private bool ShowRequestId => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(RequestId);

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        RequestId = Activity.Current?.Id ?? HttpContext?.TraceIdentifier;
}
